    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about daily routines. Interactive: Engage in role-plays based on common social situations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe daily wellness routines using appropriate vocabulary (e.g., "I walk to school every morning for exercise." "I stretch before soccer practice." "I take ten deep breaths before studying or taking a test to clear my mind and focus.")<br>Can engage in role-plays about wellness habits (e.g., pretends to explain "I do yoga because it helps me relax.")<br>Can role-play explaining a mindfulness activity (e.g., "Let's breathe deeply to relax our minds and bodies.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 25px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Mindful Minute" activity. Students close their eyes briefly and take three deep breaths, focusing on the sensation. Then, the teacher asks: "Think-Pair-Share: What's one simple thing you do to feel *calm* or *energized* during your day?" Students discuss in pairs for 2 minutes, then share one idea with the class. This activates prior knowledge about *wellness* and *routines*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing a personal *wellness habit* using target vocabulary and grammar. For example: "I usually *stretch* for five minutes every morning to *improve* my *flexibility* and *boost* my *energy*. Right now, I am *focusing* on developing a *mindfulness habit*." The teacher emphasizes stress on "flexibility" and "mindfulness." The teacher then models how to engage in a short conversation about it, asking "What about you?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and concepts related to the scenario.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I am *practicing mindfulness*,' am I talking about a finished action or something happening now?" (Expected answer: Something happening now / an ongoing action.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'meditation' an activity for your *body* or your *mental well-being*?" (Expected answer: Mental well-being, though it helps the body relax.)</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher also reviews pronunciation of "could" vs "cooed" by saying: "I *could* feel *calm* after breathing deeply." "The baby *cooed* happily after its nap."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Wellness and Mindfulness")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio clips of three different teenagers sharing their daily *wellness routines* and *mindfulness habits* (e.g., "I *walk* to school every morning for *exercise*," "I *stretch* before soccer practice," "I *take ten deep breaths* before studying to *focus* and clear my mind," "I do yoga because it helps me *relax*."). The clips will include instances of Present Simple for general statements, Present Continuous for ongoing actions, and collocations like "*energy boost*."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will listen to understand different *wellness practices* and use this as a model for talking about daily routines and engaging in social interactions related to *health* and *mental well-being*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What general topic are all three teenagers talking about?" "Are they talking about physical activities, mental activities, or both?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students listen again and match each teenager to the *wellness habit* they mention (e.g., Teenager 1: *walking*, Teenager 2: *stretching*, Teenager 3: *deep breathing/yoga*). The teacher provides a simple matching worksheet.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on using Present Simple for routines, Present Continuous for ongoing actions, collocations with health-related verbs (e.g., *energy boost*, *improve flexibility*), and target vocabulary (*wellness, mindfulness, exercise, energy, benefits, strength, flexibility, health, relax, meditate, breathe, focus, hydrate*). Pronunciation focus on stress in multisyllabic words (*flexible, flexibility, resilience, resilient, mindfulness*) and distinguishing minimal pairs (/ʊ/ vs /uː/ in *could/cooed, soot/suit*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion (5 min):</b> Students receive sentences with blanks related to wellness activities and choose the correct verb or noun from a word bank to complete them. Example: "Many people ____ (meditate/exercise) to ____ (boost/promote) their *mental well-being*." "Taking deep breaths can ____ (relax/strengthen) your *body* and *mind*." Teacher monitors and provides immediate feedback on accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Guided Sentence Building (8 min):</b> In pairs, students create sentences about specific wellness activities using provided sentence frames and picture prompts.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Frame 1: "Every day, I ____ (verb) to ____ (verb) my ____ (noun)." (e.g., "Every day, I *exercise* to *strengthen* my *body*.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Frame 2: "Right now, she/he is ____ (verb-ing) because it ____ (verb) her/him to ____ (verb)." (e.g., "Right now, she *is meditating* because it *helps* her to *focus*.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of stress in words like "flexibility" and reminds students to distinguish between /ʊ/ and /uː/ by giving example sentences: "He *could* feel better soon." "The pigeon *cooed* loudly." Students practice saying these contrasting pairs.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Wellness Routine Interview" (7 min):</b> Students interview their partner using guided questions, focusing on their *daily routines* and *wellness habits*. Questions include: "What is one *habit* you *maintain* for your *health*?" "Are you *practicing* any new *mindfulness* techniques right now?" "How does *exercise* *boost* your *energy*?" Partners take notes on each other's answers. Teacher circulates, prompts for use of target language, and corrects errors.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">20 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Wellness and Mindfulness")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Role-Play: "Wellness Advocates"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs to perform one of two role-play scenarios:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Scenario 1: Sharing Wellness Practices.</b> Student A describes their daily *wellness routine* to Student B, explaining its *benefits* for *physical* and *mental well-being*. Student B asks follow-up questions to understand the *routine* better and suggests similar activities they *could* try. (e.g., "I *exercise* three times a week to stay fit and focused." "That sounds great! I *could* start by *walking* more.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Scenario 2: Explaining a Mindfulness Activity.</b> Student A wants to introduce a friend (Student B) to a *mindfulness* activity (e.g., *meditation*, *deep breathing*). Student A explains what the activity is, how to do it (e.g., "Let's *breathe* deeply to *relax* our *minds* and *bodies*."), and its *benefits* (e.g., "it helps to *manage stress* and *improve focus*"). Student B expresses interest and asks practical questions. (e.g., "Some people like to *meditate* because it *helps* them *manage stress*." "How often do you *practice* it?")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are encouraged to use present simple, present continuous, collocations, and the target vocabulary comprehensively. Teacher circulates, listening for fluency and communicative effectiveness, providing minimal intervention.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist while students perform their role-plays from Stage 4. This focuses on their ability to talk about *daily routines* and *engage in role-plays* about *wellness habits* and *mindfulness activities*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially stress in multisyllabic words (*flexible, flexibility, resilience, mindfulness*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (*wellness, mindfulness, exercise, benefits, relax, focus, breathe, mental well-being*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Present Simple (for routines) and Present Continuous (for ongoing actions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanations and ability to elaborate on *benefits*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction and effective turn-taking in the role-play.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two short prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new *wellness habit* I learned about today that I might consider trying is... "</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel [very confident / somewhat confident / not confident] talking about the *benefits* of *mindfulness* and *wellness routines* because..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about explaining *wellness routines* or *mindfulness activities* to your partner, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use the vocabulary and phrases from today's lesson to talk about *mental* and *physical well-being* in your daily life or with your family?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the speaking activities (e.g., role-plays, interviews) worked well to promote interaction and fluency among students?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to integrate the target grammar (Present Simple/Continuous) and vocabulary (*mindfulness, benefits, relax, focus*) naturally and accurately? What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for supporting weaker students or extending stronger ones in future speaking tasks?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did the pronunciation focus (stress on multisyllabic words, minimal pairs) impact students' intelligibility and confidence in speaking?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> Today we practiced *speaking* about our *wellness routines* and *mindfulness activities*. For our next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), we will transition from oral sharing to written composition. Students will use the vocabulary and grammar practiced today to *write* short paragraphs describing their own *personal wellness plans* or *mindfulness strategies*, explaining the *benefits* of each, and sharing them with a wider audience.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>